#!/usr/bin/python3 -I
"""Wild OS restore points.

The one tool that makes, lists, prunes and applies restore points, for
Settings, the apt hooks, the kernel hook, the initramfs and start-up
recovery. See docs/recovery-design.md.

Two backends, picked from the root filesystem:
  btrfs  a read-only snapshot of @ in /.snapshots/<id>/snapshot
  ext4   an rsync copy hard-linked to the previous point, in
         /var/lib/wildos/restore-points/<id>/tree

Either way each point has info.json and a copy of /boot (kernels and
initramfs files) beside it, and an index of every point is kept
world-readable for Settings.

    restore-points list [--json]
    restore-points create [--label TEXT] [--kind manual|update|driver|first]
    restore-points delete ID
    restore-points go-back ID [--hold]       Btrfs: done now; ext4: staged
    restore-points apply-pending             ext4, from the initramfs
    restore-points restore ID --target DIR [--top DIR] [--boot DIR]
                                             from start-up recovery
    restore-points prune
    restore-points measure
    restore-points limits COUNT GB on|off
    restore-points boot                      once per start-up
    restore-points expire-holds
    restore-points apt-pre | apt-pkgs | apt-post   (apt hooks)
    restore-points kernel-removed VERSION    (kernel postrm hook)
"""

